ritish  police literally dragged assange into  custody he remains in a london prison  his lawyers say they will appeal  friday's ruling to the uk supreme court  should they fail britain's interior  minister will make the final call on his  extradition redmond shannon global news  london  the two journalists who share this  year's nobel peace prize are calling for  better protection for independent  reporting  maria resa of the philippines and  dimitri muratov of russia received their  awards today during a pomp filled  ceremony in norway in his acceptance  speech muritov called journalists the  antidote against tyranny raisa used her  speech to attack social media platforms  our greatest need today  is to transform that hate and violence  the toxic sludge that's coursing through  our information ecosystem prioritized  by american internet companies that make  more money by spreading that hate  and triggering the worst in us  the two journalists received the award  for their separate fights for freedom of  expression in countries where reporters  have faced attacks harassment and death  division over vaccination ahead how  opposing views are threatening a  family's future  you're watching global national  while the majority of eligible canadians  are now fully vaccinated against covet  19 there are still some who refuse to  get the shot so what happens when those  two sides live under the same roof  jasmine pizano has the story of an  ontario family divided  [Applause]  noah callahan could always count on his  whole family to be there for his hockey  games  that is until his dad needed a covid19  vaccine to get into the arena it hurts  you that i don't go right yes like  didn't you hurt  yeah you don't think it hurts my heart  that i can't go  no because you're making that choice  paul is the outlier in his family the  only one who's against getting  vaccinated a decision he says is rooted  in his faith in god his blood is shed  all over me  so it's like my shield but these are the  times of these kids lives that you'll  never ever get back again  and you're missing it all  you're missing it all because you've  chosen  not  to get a vaccination the first principle  to  to maintaining relationships even if you  have a difference in viewpoint is to be  non-judgmental this family doctor says  the first step for families seeking to  find common ground is setting aside  uninterrupted time to talk everyone gets  five minutes to say what they're  thinking and then the conversation ends  and come back to the table maybe three  or four days later or a week later she  says giving people that space can create  more time to reflect on their decisions  it's just crazy that some people  won't want to get it i've told them  uh that it's easy  or to get it because we'll be able to do  everything again  like  what we did in a normal life  noah is 11 years old and wants to be  vaccinated but his doctor told him to  wait he was born with a heart defect and  the vaccine could make him vulnerable to  side effects his mom and sister are  fully dosed to protect themselves and  him if you can't do it for yourself do  it for your family do it for noah we at  the as the adult are his protector  and it's up to us to protect him the  callaghans have had conversations on  this for months but nothing's changed  you just got to respect other people's  choices if they want to come out of this  pandemic as a family acceptance may be  their way forward  jasmine pizano global news trenton  ontario  next the mysterious cobia condition that  has many canadians suffering long after  infection  nearly two years into the pandemic many  questions remain around long covid the  mysterious condition in which patients  don't seem to fully recover it's  afflicting a growing number of canadians  canada's health agency says about 60  percent of those diagnosed reported at  least one symptom more than three months  after the initial infection robin gill  has been following these cases and joins  us with more robin  researchers including the team at saint  paul's hospital in vancouver have been  busy trying to understand why some  patients are still sick long after they  beat the disease are there a lot of  people still long hauling katie mclean  is receiving treatment and she's part of  a research study at st paul's  she's been suffering from long covet for  more than a year we first met her in the  fall of 2020 and have been following her  journey at one point she thought she was  getting better then she had a major  setback so i got all my original  covid symptoms from my acute infection  back yeah i had shortness of breath  chest pains palpitations extreme fatigue  you know  crazy headaches dizziness  i also lost my smell and taste again i  thought i had covet again i really  since the start of the pandemic clinics  like this one are being opened all  around the world trying to help patients  and unravel why and who is still sick  the syndrome varies with around 100  symptoms reported as far as i'm  concerned we're no closer to really  understanding definitively what the  underlying cause of this syndrome is it  seems like the virus is like picking  random people to impact  and what is it about these random people  what do they have in common  i don't know yeah it's still a mystery  to us  to try and answer that question and find  out who was more likely to suffer from  long covid we spoke with leading experts  in the field and their research so far  is offering critical clues colleen  thanks robin to hear about the  groundbreaking work scientists are doing  in the field of long covid watch robin's  full story on the new reality tomorrow  night at 7 on global  and that is global national for this  friday i'm colleen christie tonight's  your canada is this skate around the  christmas tree in point claire quebec we  love seeing your festive photos please  keep sending them to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching robin wi